Note: Dictionaries store values under text keys, like a real dictionary stores definitions under words.

Let ages be a new dictionary.
Set ages["Alice"] to 12.
Set ages["Bob"] to 14.

output ages["Alice"].
output ages["Bob"].

Note: Setting an existing key replaces its value.
Set ages["Alice"] to 13.
output ages["Alice"].

Note: sizeOf counts the entries, hasKey checks if a key exists.
output the result of sizeOf with ages.
output the result of hasKey with ages and "Bob".

Note: Remove takes an entry out.
Remove "Bob" from ages.
output the result of hasKey with ages and "Bob".

Note: Dictionaries can hold decimals or text too.
Let prices be a new dictionary from text to decimal.
Set prices["apple"] to 1.5.
Set prices["banana"] to 0.75.
output prices["apple"] + prices["banana"].

Let colors be a new dictionary from text to text.
Set colors["sky"] to "blue".
output colors["sky"].

Note: keysOf gives you every key as a list of text.
Let fruit be a list of text with value the result of keysOf with prices.
For each i from 0 to 1,
    output fruit[i].
End.
